# ─── Environment + raw signer keys ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ENV selects the deploy environment. It is normalised at load (trimmed,
# lower-cased, and "prod" is treated as "production"), so "Production",
# " production ", "PROD" etc. all count. Anything else (including unset,
# which defaults to "development") is non-production.
ENV=development
#
# Several signers (IT operator, gasless relayer, ramp gas sponsor, PM release
# local signer) are, today, a raw private key read from an env var. That is a
# dev/staging convenience only: in production it is REFUSED by default and
# the corresponding feature stays dormant (IT + utility bills, gasless
# relaying, ramp gas sponsorship, PM auto-settle, respectively) rather than
# running with a hot key that isn't in a proper key store.
#
# ALLOW_RAW_SIGNER_KEYS=true is the interim opt-in until a KMS-backed signer
# ships (see internal/services/release_signer.go — "kms" is not yet
# implemented). Setting it logs `ALERT raw_signer_key_in_production=1
# key=<NAME>` at boot for every raw key actually used, one line per key, so
# the exposure is visible in every log sink without ever logging the key
# itself.
#
# IMPORTANT — rollout: do not flip ENV to "production" on its own. Today's
# production process runs with ENV NOT EXACTLY "production" (case identified
# 2026-09), which is what currently keeps IT, utility bills, and any raw
# signer key alive. Setting ENV=production without ALLOW_RAW_SIGNER_KEYS=true
# will silently take IT, bills, gasless relaying, ramp gas sponsorship and PM
# auto-settle dormant. Set both together in the same deploy:
#   ENV=production
#   ALLOW_RAW_SIGNER_KEYS=true
# Before that flip, also review: gin is switched to ReleaseMode under
# ENV=production (quieter logs, different default error responses), and the
# nil-chain-client fallbacks in escrow_service.go, mcgp_swap_service.go,
# otc_service.go and service_contact_service.go start refusing to build a
# partial unsigned tx once ENV=production (they already do this correctly for
# a broken chain client; the concern is only environments that route through
# them without a chain client on purpose, e.g. certain test/dev tooling).
ALLOW_RAW_SIGNER_KEYS=false

# Flutterwave v4 — the platform's fiat payment gateway (Nomba declined
# production API access, 2026-07). Several pieces of IT depend on this:
# withdrawal FX quotes, per-user fiat virtual accounts, PM sell payouts, the
# webhook route, and the brokered stablecoin ramp below.

# FLUTTERWAVE_BASE_URL defaults to Flutterwave's SANDBOX API. Leaving this
# blank is NOT the same as leaving it unset: getEnv (config.go) uses
# os.LookupEnv, so a bare `FLUTTERWAVE_BASE_URL=` line still counts as "set"
# (to ""), which defeats this compiled default — the client
# (flutterwave_client.go, fwDefaultSandboxBase) then re-defaults it back to
# sandbox anyway, silently, with no warning log. PRODUCTION MUST set this
# explicitly to Flutterwave's production base URL. Do not invent that URL
# here: it was mid-migration on Flutterwave's side as of 2026-08 and must be
# confirmed with Flutterwave at go-live, not guessed.
FLUTTERWAVE_BASE_URL=https://developersandbox-api.flutterwave.com
# Environment-neutral — same IDP host for sandbox and production.
FLUTTERWAVE_IDP_URL=https://idp.flutterwave.com
# Absent client id/secret ⇒ NewFlutterwaveClient returns nil and the whole FW
# client is dormant: withdrawal FX quotes, virtual accounts, and PM sell
# payouts all error until both are set.
FLUTTERWAVE_CLIENT_ID=
FLUTTERWAVE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Absent ⇒ the Flutterwave webhook route stays dormant (main.go only
# registers it once this is set).
FLUTTERWAVE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Absent ⇒ the brokered stablecoin ramp below stays dormant, regardless of
# FW_STABLE_DEPOSIT_ADDRESS_POLYGON.
FLUTTERWAVE_MERCHANT_ID=
# Optional — parents every Flutterwave virtual account. Left blank, one is
# minted lazily on boot and persisted per environment, so operators needn't
# dig it out of the dashboard by hand.
FLUTTERWAVE_CUSTOMER_ID=
FLUTTERWAVE_COMPANY_NAME=TSA Connect
FLUTTERWAVE_COMPANY_EMAIL=payments@mcgpchain.com
FLUTTERWAVE_COMPANY_PHONE=

# ─── Central-account inbound attribution ────────────────────────────────────
#
# INBOUND_MODE selects where payers are sent. "va" is the legacy behaviour: a
# per-user Flutterwave virtual account, attributed by the charge webhook.
# "central" routes every payer to the one company account below, where
# attribution instead comes from polling the wallet statement and matching the
# sender's name against their Didit-verified legal name.
#
# It is a single global flag on purpose. A per-user rollout would put two live
# attribution paths over one account, and a credit that both or neither claims.
#
# Flipping it does NOT close the virtual accounts already minted: nothing
# deactivates them at Flutterwave, and the charge webhook still credits them, so
# a payer who saved an old account number keeps being credited that way. Central
# mode only stops new accounts being minted and stops the app showing the old
# ones. Retiring them at the provider is a separate, deliberate exercise.
# Reverting is this variable plus a restart.
INBOUND_MODE=va

# The company account every payer is sent to in central mode. REQUIRED when
# INBOUND_MODE=central — boot refuses to start without all three, because a
# pay-into screen with a blank account number is a user transferring money
# nowhere. The number is checked as a 10-digit NUBAN; that cannot catch a
# transposed digit, only the bank can, so check it against the dashboard.
INBOUND_CENTRAL_ACCOUNT_NUMBER=
INBOUND_CENTRAL_BANK_NAME=
INBOUND_CENTRAL_ACCOUNT_NAME=

# Wallet funding fires NO webhook, so this poll is the only way an inbound
# credit is ever noticed, and the interval is the payer's wait. Also required
# in central mode: boot refuses without Flutterwave credentials, since without
# them nothing reads the account at all.
INBOUND_POLL_INTERVAL=30s
# Min gap between a finished statement poll and one triggered by a user tapping "I've sent the transfer" (global, not per user).
INBOUND_CHECK_COOLDOWN=10s
# How far back each poll reads. Overlap is free — ingestion is idempotent on the
# NIBSS session id — whereas a window shorter than an outage silently loses every
# credit that arrived during it. Deliberately far wider than the interval.
INBOUND_STATEMENT_LOOKBACK=24h

# Operators alerted on every held credit. Comma-separated. A held credit is
# customer money nobody can be shown to own, and by design nothing releases it
# automatically: no expiry, no auto-refund. Left blank, holds still queue at
# /api/admin/inbound/credits but nobody is paged, and the queue is only found by
# someone choosing to open it.
# The same list is also emailed when a money-path background job (inbound poller
# and reconciler, IT sell/buy/stable workers, bill reconciler, P2P chain
# reconciler, PM recovery, settlement forward sweep, Paycrest poller, ramp
# conversion) fails every run for ~5 minutes — hourly while it stays down, and
# once when it recovers. Blank leaves the `[jobs] ALERT worker_failing_total=1`
# log line as the only signal.
INBOUND_ALERT_EMAILS=

# Stablecoin ⇄ fiat ramp (brokered through Flutterwave; no float is held)
IT_STABLE_SPREAD_BPS=155
IT_STABLE_RATE_LOCK_SECONDS=900
# Flutterwave's own Polygon deposit address — dashboard-only, copied by hand.
FW_STABLE_DEPOSIT_ADDRESS_POLYGON=
# How far a deposit may exceed the amount it was quoted for and still pay out
# automatically. Beyond it the sell is parked for an operator: the payout scales
# with what actually arrived, so an unbounded ratio would let a small quote
# claim an arbitrarily large deposit.
IT_STABLE_OVER_DELIVERY_TOL_BPS=200
# Provider floor for a sell, in major units. Flutterwave rejects a conversion
# below its own minimum AFTER the user has irreversibly sent funds, so the quote
# refuses first — the last point where refusing is still free. The app enforces
# the same number, but that is a usability guard: a client-side check does not
# protect a direct API caller. Leaving this blank or malformed keeps the built-in
# default; the floor can be raised or lowered, never removed.
IT_STABLE_MIN_SELL=10

# ── IT sell recovery: trust "no transfer at Flutterwave" ──
# The sell reconciler and the admin sell actions look payouts up by reference
# in Flutterwave's GET /transfers listing. When that listing says a reference
# has no transfer, true lets them act on it: the reconciler re-sends a payout
# that was already attempted (same reference) and reverses a sell whose retry
# was refused, and an admin may reverse or retry-payout such a sell.
# Default false. The listing's response shape was built from Flutterwave's
# published v4 schema and has NOT been confirmed against the live API; a
# misread "absent" pays the user twice (naira sent AND MCGP returned). While
# false those sells only ALERT and appear in GET /api/it/admin/sell/stuck.
# Provider-confirmed outcomes (a transfer found SUCCESSFUL/FAILED) and first
# sends of payouts that were provably never sent work either way.
IT_SELL_FW_ABSENCE_TRUSTED=false

# ── OTC desk sell cap ──
# A wallet may sell back on the OTC desk only what it bought there, rebuilt
# from the OtcMarketplace's Bought/Sold logs. Unset OTC_MARKETPLACE_ADDRESS
# refuses every OTC sell. The deploy block defaults to the mainnet desk's
# creation block (60909790) for 0xfEF5…BF8b and to 0 (genesis) otherwise.
# The max range is only the first eth_getLogs span tried (0 = whole range);
# the scanner halves on refusal, so RPCs with a range cap still work.
OTC_MARKETPLACE_ADDRESS=
OTC_MARKETPLACE_DEPLOY_BLOCK=
OTC_LOG_MAX_BLOCK_RANGE=0

# ── Spendable MCGP checkout abuse bounds ──
# Paying from Spendable MCGP and refunding it costs the buyer nothing, but every
# round sends operator transactions and uses the contract's rolling debit/credit
# windows, which bills and IT share (a reverse never gives window usage back).
# Unfinished Spendable payments one buyer may hold at once:
ORDER_MCGP_MAX_OPEN_HOLDS=3
# Refunds/cancels in 24h after which the buyer can't START another Spendable
# payment (refunds themselves are never refused — that would trap the MCGP):
ORDER_MCGP_MAX_REFUNDS_PER_DAY=5
# Percent of debitWindowCap / creditWindowCap checkout may use (measured from
# order_mcgp_payments); the rest is reserved for bills and IT. 1..100.
ORDER_MCGP_WINDOW_SHARE_PCT=30

# ── EVM chain RPCs (<CHAIN>_MAINNET_RPC_URL / <CHAIN>_TESTNET_RPC_URL) ──
# Each takes one URL or a comma-separated failover list, primary first. With a
# list, a request the active endpoint answers with 429, 5xx or a transport
# error is retried on the next one, which stays active for 60s before the
# primary is tried again (log line: "[rpc] chain=<id> endpoint <host> -> <host>",
# hosts only). A JSON-RPC error inside a 200 and any other 4xx are real answers
# and never fail over. A broadcast moves on only after a 429. Every entry must
# be http(s). Put keyed URLs first; the key never reaches a log line.
# Defaults (used when unset):
# BASE_MAINNET_RPC_URL=https://developer-access-mainnet.base.org,https://mainnet.base.org,https://base.gateway.tenderly.co
# POLYGON_MAINNET_RPC_URL=https://polygon-bor-rpc.publicnode.com,https://rpc-mainnet.matic.quiknode.pro,https://polygon.drpc.org

# ── Ramp gas sponsorship (Paycrest sells on base/arbitrum/polygon/bsc pay no gas) ──
# Off unless both are set. The key must NOT be the relayer, IT operator or PM release key.
RAMP_GAS_SPONSORSHIP=false
RAMP_SPONSOR_PRIVATE_KEY=
RAMP_SPONSOR_CHAINS=base,arbitrum,polygon,bsc
# Gas units per UTC day, per chain. A runaway-bug backstop (~4k relays/day at
# ~120k gas each); the balance floor and float alert protect the money.
RAMP_SPONSOR_DAILY_GAS_BUDGET=500000000
# Gas top-ups a user may leave UNUSED (order expired/cancelled/failed with
# nothing paid) in a rolling 24h. Top-ups that led to a deposit never count.
RAMP_SPONSOR_TOPUP_UNUSED_DAILY_CAP=2
# Per-chain overrides (wei). Defaults: floor ~$5, max single top-up ~$0.10-0.30.
# RAMP_SPONSOR_BALANCE_FLOOR_WEI_BASE=2000000000000000
# RAMP_SPONSOR_TOPUP_MAX_WEI_BSC=500000000000000

# ── Marketplace checkout: pay with another token ──
# The buyer's token (any EVM chain) is swapped through LiFi into the order
# token (Sonic USDC/USDT) in HER OWN wallet, then the unchanged escrow is paid
# from it. Pass-through: we take no fee; she pays LiFi's cost + the gas we pay
# for her, and is refused only when her wallet can't cover the route. Off by default
# (flag-off-first); source-chain top-ups for the swap use the ramp sponsor lanes
# above exactly as a sale's do.
MARKETPLACE_FUNDING=false
# Gas one order's approve + createOrder takes on Sonic; sizes the S top-up.
MARKETPLACE_ESCROW_GAS_PER_ORDER=400000
# The funding's OWN Sonic gas lane: S sent after the swap lands to a verified
# buyer who holds none, so she can sign each order's approve + createOrder.
# Deliberately NOT a RAMP_SPONSOR_CHAINS lane (that would also top up sells and
# TSA Pay intents from Sonic). Signs with RAMP_SPONSOR_PRIVATE_KEY under its own
# guard; keep that key funded with S. Off: a buyer with no S is refused before
# she signs anything ("add a little S"). ~400k gas x2 at ~55 gwei is ~0.045 S an
# order; sponsoring stops below the floor, the ceiling covers a ~10-order cart.
MARKETPLACE_SONIC_GAS=false
# MARKETPLACE_SONIC_GAS_FLOOR_WEI=5000000000000000000
# MARKETPLACE_SONIC_GAS_TOPUP_MAX_WEI=500000000000000000

# ── Ramp treasury / converted buys (Paycrest → our treasury → LiFi → user's own chain/token) ──
# Off unless both are set. The key must NOT be the relayer, IT operator, PM
# release, or ramp sponsor key — the treasury holds USER FUNDS in transit, so
# sharing its key with any other hot signer is refused at boot.
RAMP_CONVERSION_BUY=false
RAMP_TREASURY_PRIVATE_KEY=
RAMP_TREASURY_CHAINS=base,arbitrum,polygon,bsc,optimism,avalanche,linea,sonic,ethereum
# Paycrest's own onramp cap per order, and this chain's total in-flight
# exposure across every converted buy the treasury hasn't yet settled.
RAMP_TREASURY_MAX_INFLIGHT_USD_PER_ORDER=2000
RAMP_TREASURY_MAX_INFLIGHT_USD_PER_CHAIN=10000
# Per-chain low-balance warning (wei): below it a lane logs a refill ALERT.
# Not a stop — a lane keeps working while it can pay for the transaction in
# hand, and converted routes stop being OFFERED once it cannot. Defaults are
# placeholders around one bridge tx (~$5-10 of native gas); retune once real
# bridge gas costs are observed in production.
# RAMP_TREASURY_NATIVE_FLOOR_WEI_BASE=5000000000000000
# RAMP_TREASURY_NATIVE_FLOOR_WEI_ETHEREUM=10000000000000000
# Contracts a LiFi route may make the treasury call or approve. Defaults pin
# LiFi's diamond per chain (read from li.quest/v1/chains; Linea has its own).
# Override only when LiFi migrates a diamond — comma-separated. A chain whose
# list is empty or holds a malformed address gets no lane at boot.
# RAMP_TREASURY_ROUTERS_BSC=0x1231DEB6f5749EF6cE6943a275A1D3E7486F4EaE
# Decision 10: how long a converted buy may sit with no broadcast bridge
# attempt before it is refunded instead of retried again.
RAMP_CONVERSION_RETRY_MINUTES=30
# Decision 6: smallest converted-buy order, measured on the settlement leg's
# stablecoin amount. Ethereum/Linea override higher — their gas cost makes a
# $1 order not worth converting.
RAMP_CONVERSION_MIN_USD=1
RAMP_CONVERSION_MIN_USD_ETHEREUM=5
RAMP_CONVERSION_MIN_USD_LINEA=25
# ConversionOrchestrator tunables — shown at their code defaults (6h stall
# window, 24h hard leg ceiling, $50 automatic top-up ceiling). Override to
# retune without a redeploy.
# RAMP_CONVERSION_STALL_MINUTES=360
# RAMP_CONVERSION_LEG_CEILING_HOURS=24
# RAMP_CONVERSION_MAX_SHORTFALL_USD=50
# USD threshold above which the treasury float monitor alerts that a lane's
# kept USDC (the round trip's unspent budget from converted buys) needs a
# manual sweep. Sweeping itself is still manual in Phase 1.
RAMP_TREASURY_KEPT_USDC_ALERT=500
# Company wallet — the company sells its own USDC/USDT as a ramp buy provider
# (spec 2026-10-03 §4.1). RAMP_TREASURY_BUY is the provider's kill switch, default
# off; turn on after the app update is adopted. Off stops new quotes and opens only:
# with the key set, open orders still settle and deliver. The key MUST differ from
# every other key in this file (fatal).
RAMP_TREASURY_BUY=false
RAMP_TREASURY_WALLET_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Chains it sells on; each needs a lane, an RPC that serves receipts, and readable decimals.
RAMP_TREASURY_WALLET_CHAINS=base
# Per-order maximum, in USD (= tokens), on quote, open and live pricing.
RAMP_TREASURY_MAX_USD=200
# Converted venues (spec 2026-10-03 §15.3): a naira buy of an asset no provider
# delivers directly, filled by a USDC leg into the conversion treasury and bridged
# by LiFi. No switch of their own — each follows its provider (gates quoting and
# opening only; open orders always finish):
# Route 1 (company wallet) is on with RAMP_TREASURY_BUY + its key, RAMP_CONVERSION_BUY
# and a conversion-treasury lane (RAMP_TREASURY_CHAINS) on a company-wallet chain.
# Route 2 (Flutterwave) is on with RAMP_FLUTTERWAVE_BUY, RAMP_CONVERSION_BUY and a Polygon
# conversion-treasury lane whose USDC is the one Flutterwave sends (bundled in
# internal/config; a mismatch keeps route 2 off with ALERT ramp_fw_converted_token_mismatch).
# How long a Flutterwave delivery reported SUCCESSFUL with no on-chain hash waits for
# one before it parks for an operator.
RAMP_FLUTTERWAVE_HASH_WAIT_MINUTES=10

# ── Swapped sells (her token → LiFi → USDC on Base in HER wallet → offramp) ──
# Off by default. Independent of RAMP_CONVERSION_BUY: no treasury key is
# involved, since her funds never pass through us. Needs a LiFi integrator fee
# wallet on each source chain, or the sell for that chain is simply not offered.
RAMP_CONVERSION_SELL=false
# The EIP-3009 relay on Base we sponsor to move her landed USDC into the
# offramp, in USD. Part of g, the gas we pay, passed on at cost.
RAMP_SELL_SPONSORED_GAS_USD=0.02
# Ramp router (provider-neutral buy/sell, /api/ramp/*).
# Tolerance for "no worse than shown", in bps of the quoted rate.
RAMP_PRICE_TOLERANCE_BPS=50
# log = record ramp_price_guard_would_refuse only; enforce = refuse the order.
RAMP_PRICE_GUARD=log
RAMP_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT=8s
RAMP_OPEN_ATTEMPT_TIMEOUT=15s
RAMP_OPEN_BUDGET=20s
# ── Ramp buy providers (spec 2026-10-03 rev 3) ──
# Flutterwave buys and sends each order — Polygon only, BUY only. Kill switch:
# turn on only after the app update is adopted and the canary passed.
RAMP_FLUTTERWAVE_BUY=false
# Smallest order, in tokens DELIVERED (after every fee line).
RAMP_FLUTTERWAVE_MIN_TOKENS=10
# Largest order, in USD (= tokens); quotes and opens only, never live pricing.
RAMP_FLUTTERWAVE_MAX_USD=2000
# Flutterwave's conversion fee, passed on at cost as its own fee line (measured 2026-10-05).
RAMP_FLUTTERWAVE_CONVERSION_FEE_BPS=150
# Flutterwave's crypto send fee, passed on at cost as "Network fee" (measured 2026-10-05).
RAMP_FLUTTERWAVE_SEND_FEE_USD=1.50
# Where the send fee is taken: wallet (on top) | amount (inside; we send amount + fee). Set from the canary.
RAMP_FLUTTERWAVE_SEND_FEE_MODE=wallet
# Shared by both providers: unpaid routed buys a user may abandon per rolling 24h,
# and the processing grace past the 15-minute honour window (IT_STABLE_RATE_LOCK_SECONDS).
RAMP_DAILY_ABANDON_CAP=5
RAMP_HONOUR_GRACE_SECONDS=600
# Our only margin on every ramp order, in bps (owner ruling 2026-10-07): the
# company wallet and Flutterwave (the shown rate is the market, or
# Flutterwave's rate, × (1 + take)) and Paycrest, buy and sell, direct,
# converted or swapped, where it is sent as the sender fee. One rule for every
# provider: 1..500; anything else (0, negative, larger) is the default 50 with
# a boot ALERT ramp_company_take_invalid.
# Third-party costs are passed on at cost as fee lines, never folded into it.
# /api/it/stable/buy keeps IT_STABLE_SPREAD_BPS; TSA Pay keeps TSA_PAY_FEE_PERCENT.
RAMP_COMPANY_TAKE_BPS=50
# Funding fee, at cost, on both in-house buys: the cost of the channel her
# naira arrives through. Central account (Flutterwave wallet funding) measured
# 1.40%; her virtual account (a collection, 2.00% + VAT) measured 2.15%. The
# defaults are right — override only if Flutterwave changes them.
# RAMP_FUNDING_FEE_BPS=140
# RAMP_VA_FUNDING_FEE_BPS=215
# Company wallet pricing and limits (its key and chains are with RAMP_TREASURY_BUY above).
RAMP_TREASURY_MIN_NGN=100
RAMP_TREASURY_DAILY_USER_MAX_USD=500
# Free stock per token below which operators are emailed (hourly at most).
RAMP_TREASURY_LOW_BALANCE_USD=50
# A send with no on-chain proof after this many minutes alerts; nothing re-sends it automatically.
RAMP_TREASURY_SEND_STALL_MINUTES=30
# Company-wallet pricing only: Paycrest's reference rate and the Flutterwave mid must agree
# within this many bps (1..1000; else 400), or a lone source with the FX feed, else its leg closes.
RAMP_TREASURY_RATE_TOL_BPS=400
# A company-wallet sale settles only once its receipt is this many blocks deep.
RAMP_TREASURY_CONFIRMATIONS=5
# Most a converted buy or swapped sell may pass on at cost, in bps, on top of
# the take: the LiFi round trip plus the gas we pay (a buy adds 10 bps
# headroom; a sell's LiFi fee is exactly the gas we pay). A route needing more
# is not offered. Per chain (a buy's destination, a sell's source):
# RAMP_CONVERTED_FEE_MAX_BPS_<CHAIN> (e.g. _BSC, _SOLANA). 0 turns that chain's
# converted buys and swapped sells off;
# anything outside 1..500, or not a whole number, is read as 0. TSA Pay's
# converted payouts are capped by it too (on their whole fee, as before).
RAMP_CONVERTED_FEE_MAX_BPS=200

# ── LiFi swap aggregator (AllSwap, ramp-conversion treasury, IT stable verifier) ──
# API key is optional — without it requests hit LiFi's anonymous, rate-limited
# tier. Integrator + fee are also optional; setting both collects a percentage
# cut, which requires a matching registration at portal.li.fi with a fee
# wallet configured.
LIFI_API_KEY=
LIFI_INTEGRATOR_ID=
LIFI_INTEGRATOR_FEE=
# LIFI_BASE_URL overrides LiFi's public API base (defaults to
# https://li.quest/v1) for every LiFi client in the backend. It exists solely
# so a LOCAL REHEARSAL can point at a stand-in server: the real LiFi API can
# never satisfy a status check for a converted buy whose transaction only
# exists on a local chain fork. LEAVE UNSET IN PRODUCTION. A blank or
# whitespace-only value falls back to the default rather than being used
# verbatim.
LIFI_BASE_URL=

# ── Name change by re-verification (verified users) + TSA Pay business rename ──
# Off by default so the API can deploy dark. While off, users cannot START a
# name change and the owner business-name route is not registered. Everything
# about a change already running — paying, verifying, cancelling, the Didit
# webhook, admin review and the sweeper that settles/returns fees — keeps
# working, so switching it off never strands a fee.
NAME_CHANGE_ENABLED=false
# The re-verification fee in USD, a plain decimal (at most 6 places). Read when
# a change is created and frozen on it. 0 charges nothing. A value that does not
# parse stops new changes (boot logs an ALERT) rather than charging a guess.
# Paid from spendable MCGP (needs the IT operator signer) or USDT/USDC into the
# ramp treasury (needs RAMP_CONVERSION_BUY and its treasury lanes).
NAME_CHANGE_FEE_USD=1.00
# Completed changes a user may make in any rolling 12 months.
NAME_CHANGE_MAX_PER_YEAR=2
# Didit sessions one paid change may use (Didit bills per session), and the days
# from payment within which they must be used. After either the change lapses
# and a new one needs a new fee.
NAME_CHANGE_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
NAME_CHANGE_TTL_DAYS=7
# Didit sessions (each one billed) a user may start in any 30 days, however
# their changes were paid for — a zero fee does not make sessions free to burn.
NAME_CHANGE_MAX_SESSIONS_PER_30_DAYS=6
# Fees returned to a user (cancel before verifying, or an unused change lapsing)
# per window; past it a cancel is refused and an unused lapse keeps the fee.
# Stops a cancel-and-reopen loop from spending treasury/operator gas. Minimum 1.
NAME_CHANGE_MAX_REFUNDED_CANCELS=1
NAME_CHANGE_REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS=30
# Hold every change for an admin unless Didit's face search matched the new
# selfie to the account's ORIGINAL KYC session (liveness_checks[].matches).
# Needs face search / duplicate detection switched on in the Didit workflow;
# without it every change is held. Leave true unless an admin reviews them all anyway.
NAME_CHANGE_REQUIRE_FACE_MATCH=true
# Blocks deep a stablecoin fee must be before it counts. Per treasury chain:
# NAME_CHANGE_MIN_CONFIRMATIONS_<CHAIN> (defaults: polygon 10, bsc 5).
NAME_CHANGE_MIN_CONFIRMATIONS=3
# The smallest stablecoin amount (USD) sent back on its own: a payment that
# could not pay a change (short, or after it closed) or the overpaid part of a
# kept fee. Less is kept and logged for support (name_change_bounce_kept_total
# for a bounce, name_change_excess_kept_total for an overpayment).
# Such "bounce" refunds also count toward NAME_CHANGE_MAX_REFUNDED_CANCELS —
# except a payment our side stopped waiting for (dropped as unknown to the
# chain, or cleared by an admin) that lands later: that goes back whole,
# whatever its size, and counts toward nothing.
NAME_CHANGE_MIN_REFUND_USD=0.50
# Words no TSA Pay business name may contain (case-insensitive, spaces and
# lookalike letters ignored), comma-separated. ADDED to the built-in list —
# TSA Connect, TSAConnect, TSA Pay, TSAPay, support, refund — never replacing it.
TSA_PAY_RESERVED_BUSINESS_NAMES=

# ── TSA Pay ──
# Merchant payments platform (off by default; live mode off until licensing is answered).
TSA_PAY_ENABLED=false
TSA_PAY_LIVE_ENABLED=false
# ≥ 32 random bytes as hex; rotating it re-keys every merchant's webhook secrets.
TSA_PAY_WEBHOOK_MASTER_KEY=
# Public checkout URL; defaults to https://tsaconnectworld.com/pay
TSA_PAY_CHECKOUT_BASE_URL=https://tsaconnectworld.com/pay
# Crypto refunds (merchant-funded USDT/USDC, signed in the owner's app). Off by
# default: every refund route answers 503 refunds_unavailable and the refund
# confirmer does not run; payment views and webhooks still show refunds already
# made. Before turning it on, repoint any chain RPC that refuses receipt reads
# (publicnode on Base/BSC/Arbitrum/Optimism) and check one eth_getTransactionReceipt
# per chain from the prod box — on such a chain a refund pays the customer but
# never confirms.
TSA_PAY_REFUNDS=false
# TSA Pay's own pricing, unchanged by the ramp's move to RAMP_COMPANY_TAKE_BPS.
# F, in percent: the platform fee a payment is split by and the Paycrest sender
# fee on its direct payout (KeepSurplus may raise it to keep the deposit's
# cushion). Default 1.
TSA_PAY_FEE_PERCENT=1
# B, in bps: the part of F a converted payment's swap may spend (LiFi + our
# gas); its payout is sent F − B and the unspent rest is our LiFi fee. Needs
# 0 < B < F, else converted payments are refused. Default 50.
TSA_PAY_ROUND_TRIP_BUDGET_BPS=50

# TSA Connect web app origin (served same-origin with /api behind nginx).
# Unset = web sign-in routes are off.
WEB_APP_ORIGIN=
# The web app is served by Netlify, which proxies /api here and signs each
# proxied request (x-nf-sign) with this secret. Set it to the same value as the
# Netlify site's API_SIGNATURE_TOKEN env var (scope: Runtime). With it, a signed
# request counts as coming from the visitor (X-Nf-Client-Connection-Ip), so
# per-IP rate limits don't lump every web user into Netlify's address.
# Unset while WEB_APP_ORIGIN is set logs `[web] ALERT netlify_proxy_secret_unset=1`
# at boot (it never refuses to boot); a visitor address arriving with a missing or
# invalid signature logs `netlify_client_ip_untrusted=1`, at most once a minute.
NETLIFY_PROXY_SECRET=

# CoinGecko prices (wallet balances, swap valuation, token charts). Unset = the
# keyless public API, whose per-IP limit throttles the server after a burst and
# blanks charts. A free Demo key (coingecko.com/en/api) is sent as
# x-cg-demo-api-key; set COINGECKO_API_PLAN=pro for a paid key, which uses
# pro-api.coingecko.com and x-cg-pro-api-key. Boot logs which mode is active.
COINGECKO_API_KEY=
COINGECKO_API_PLAN=
